《The Biographies of Zhang Shi-zhi and Feng Tang》

Zhang Ting-wei, whose given name was Shi-zhi, was from Duyang. His courtesy name was Ji. He had an older brother named Zhong, with whom he lived. He obtained the position of Qilang through recommendation and served Emperor Xiaowen. For ten years, he was not promoted, and he was not known for anything. Shi-zhi said, "My long service in the court has diminished my brother's wealth; it is not right." He wanted to resign and return home. Yuan Ang, a Middle Guard General, recognized his talent and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shi-zhi be transferred to the position of attendant. After Shi-zhi completed his duties for the day, he took the opportunity to offer some advice. Emperor Xiaowen said, "Treat him with respect, but do not let him make overly grand pronouncements, and let us see if his suggestions are practical." Thus, Shi-zhi spoke of the affairs of the Qin and Han dynasties, and how the Qin dynasty failed and the Han dynasty prospered. Emperor Xiaowen praised him and appointed Shi-zhi as an attendant and a member of the Secretariat.

Shi-zhi accompanied the emperor on an inspection tour, arriving at the Tiger Enclosure. The emperor asked the officer in charge of the Upper Forest about the records of the various animals, and asked more than ten questions. The officer and his assistants looked at each other, but none of them could answer. The caretaker of the Tiger Enclosure stepped forward and answered the emperor's questions about the animals in detail, intending to demonstrate his ability to respond quickly and comprehensively. Emperor Xiaowen said, "It is not appropriate for an official to behave in this way! The officer is incompetent!" He then ordered Shi-zhi to recommend the caretaker for the position of officer in charge of the Upper Forest. Shi-zhi had previously said, "Your Majesty, what do you think of Marquis of Chichou, Zhou Bo?" The emperor replied, "He is an elder." Shi-zhi then asked, "What about Marquis of Dongyang, Zhang Xiang?" The emperor replied again, "He is an elder." Shi-zhi said, "Since Marquis of Chichou and Marquis of Dongyang are considered elders, yet neither of them could articulate their views on important matters, how can this caretaker, with his quick wit and eloquent speech, be of any real value? Furthermore, the Qin dynasty relied on officials skilled in writing and record-keeping, and these officials competed with each other by being strict and meticulous, but this only led to an increase in paperwork and a lack of genuine concern for the people. As a result, no one dared to point out their mistakes, and the dynasty declined and eventually fell into the hands of the Second Emperor, leading to the collapse of the empire. Now, Your Majesty is promoting this caretaker based on his eloquence, and I fear that this will encourage others to prioritize eloquence over substance." Emperor Xiaowen said, "That is a good point." He then decided not to promote the caretaker.

The emperor got into his carriage and summoned Shi-zhi to ride with him. As they traveled, the emperor asked Shi-zhi about the weaknesses of the Qin dynasty. Shi-zhi explained them in detail. When they arrived at the palace, the emperor appointed Shi-zhi as the officer in charge of the imperial carriages.

Not long after, the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang traveled together in a carriage to attend court, but they did not stop at the Sima Gate. Shi-zhi pursued them and stopped them, preventing them from entering the palace gate. He then filed a report accusing them of disrespect for the imperial court. Empress Dowager Bo heard about this and, upon learning that Emperor Xiaowen had removed his crown and bowed in apology, saying, "I will teach my sons to be more respectful," she ordered an emissary to pardon the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang, after which they were allowed to enter. Emperor Xiaowen was impressed by Shi-zhi and appointed him as a Middle Minister.

Not long after, he was promoted to Middle Guard General. During an inspection tour to Baling, he was assigned a residence near the latrine. At that time, Lady Shen was also accompanying the emperor. The emperor pointed out the road to Xinfeng to Lady Shen, saying, "This is the road to Handan." He then asked Lady Shen to play the zither, and the emperor played along, singing a mournful song that expressed his deep sorrow and sadness. He turned to the assembled officials and said, "Alas! To use stones from the northern mountains as the coffin, to stuff it with cotton and silk, and to lacquer the inside – how can this be disturbed!" Everyone agreed, saying, "That is a good idea." Shi-zhi stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable inside, even if it is locked up in the southern mountains, there will be a way to get to it. But if there is nothing desirable inside, even if it is protected by a stone coffin, what difference will it make?" Emperor Xiaowen praised him. Later, Shi-zhi was appointed as the Chief Justice.

Not long after, the emperor traveled out of the city and crossed the Middle Wei Bridge. A man emerged from under the bridge, and the emperor's horses were startled. The emperor ordered the cavalry to capture the man and hand him over to the Chief Justice for investigation. Shi-zhi investigated the matter. He found that the man was a local resident who, upon hearing the emperor's procession, had hidden under the bridge. After a while, he thought the procession had passed, so he came out, and when he saw the emperor's carriage and horses, he ran away. The Chief Justice, Qin Dang, ruled that the man should be fined for disturbing the emperor. Emperor Xiaowen was angry and said, "This man startled my horses, and my horses are gentle. What if he had startled other horses? They might have injured me! And yet, the Chief Justice wants to impose a fine!" Shi-zhi said, "The law is what the emperor shares with the people of the world. If the law is already like this, and you want to make it even stricter, then the law will not be respected by the people. Furthermore, at the time, the emperor could have ordered the man to be executed. Now that the case has been referred to the Chief Justice, the Chief Justice represents justice for the entire world. If the Chief Justice makes a ruling that is too harsh, it will set a precedent, and all future rulings will be influenced by this. The people will be confused and unsure of what to do. Your Majesty should consider this." After a long pause, the emperor said, "The Chief Justice is right."

Later, someone stole a jade ring from the front of the High Temple. The thief was caught, and Emperor Xiaowen was angry. He ordered the Chief Justice to investigate the matter. Shi-zhi reviewed the law, which stated that anyone who stole items from a temple or imperial palace should be executed. He submitted a report recommending the death penalty. The emperor was furious and said, "This man has committed an act of disrespect by stealing from the temple of the former emperor. I wanted the Chief Justice to recommend that his entire family be punished, but you are recommending the death penalty based on the law. This is not what I want." Shi-zhi removed his crown and bowed his head in apology, saying, "The law is sufficient. Furthermore, the severity of the punishment should be proportionate to the crime. Now, if we punish the thief's entire family for stealing from the temple, it would be an extreme measure. If a foolish person were to take a handful of soil from the imperial tomb, what punishment would be appropriate?" After a while, Emperor Xiaowen discussed the matter with the Empress Dowager, who agreed that the Chief Justice's recommendation was appropriate. At that time, Zhou Yafu, the Middle Guard General, and Wang Tian, the Marquis of Liang, met with Shi-zhi and discussed the importance of impartiality. They became close friends. Zhang Ting-wei became famous throughout the empire as a result.

Later, Emperor Xiaowen died, and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shi-zhi was afraid and pretended to be ill. He wanted to resign, fearing that he would be severely punished, but he also did not know how to explain his actions. He followed the advice of Wang Sheng and finally went to pay his respects to Emperor Jing, who treated him with the same respect as before.

Wang Sheng was a scholar who was well-versed in the teachings of Huang Lao. He was invited to live in the imperial court, where he was consulted by the Three Dukes and Nine Ministers. Wang Sheng, an old man, said, "I am not very knowledgeable," and turned to Zhang Ting-wei, saying, "Please help me." Shi-zhi knelt and bowed to him. After this, some people said to Wang Sheng, "Why did you humiliate Zhang Ting-wei by making him kneel and bow to you?" Wang Sheng replied, "I am old and insignificant, and I believe that I will not be of any benefit to Zhang Ting-wei. Zhang Ting-wei is a famous minister in the empire today, so I simply humiliated him by making him kneel and bow to me, in order to show my respect for him." The officials heard this and praised Wang Sheng for his wisdom and respected Zhang Ting-wei even more.

Zhang Ting-wei served Emperor Jing for over a year, and then became the prime minister of the Principality of Huainan, where he continued to uphold his principles. After a while, Shi-zhi died. His son, Zhang Zhi, whose courtesy name was Changgong, became an official and rose to the rank of minister, but he was eventually dismissed. Because he could not adapt to the ways of the world, he spent the rest of his life in seclusion.

Feng Tang, whose great-grandfather was from Zhao. His father moved to Dai. After the Han dynasty was established, they moved to Anling. Tang was known for his filial piety and became the head of the Middle Guard, serving Emperor Xiaowen. When the emperor's carriage passed by, he asked Tang, "You are an old man, why are you serving as a minor official? Where is your home?" Tang gave a detailed account. Emperor Xiaowen said, "When I was in Dai, I often heard Gao Qi, a general, speak of the merits of Li Qi, a general of Zhao, who fought at the Battle of Julu. Even now, when I eat, I often think of Julu. Does your father know about this?" Tang replied, "He is not as knowledgeable as Lian Po and Li Mu." The emperor asked, "Why?" Tang replied, "My great-grandfather served as an officer in Zhao and admired Li Mu. My father was the prime minister of Dai and admired Li Qi, so he knew about their qualities." After hearing about Lian Po and Li Mu, the emperor was pleased and slapped his thigh, saying, "Alas! If only I had Lian Po and Li Mu to serve as my generals, I would not have to worry about the Xiongnu!" Tang said, "Your Majesty, even if you had Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them effectively." The emperor was angry and went back to his palace. After a while, he summoned Tang and said, "Why did you insult me in public? Is there no place where you can speak freely?" Tang apologized, saying, "I am ignorant of court etiquette."

At that time, the Xiongnu had recently sent an envoy to the court, and they had killed the officer in charge of the North Territory. The emperor was concerned about the threat of the Xiongnu, so he asked Tang again, "Why do you say that I would not be able to use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied, "I have heard that in ancient times, when kings appointed generals, they would kneel and offer them the reins of their chariot, saying, 'Within this area, I will make the decisions; outside this area, you will make the decisions.' All military matters, including rewards and promotions, would be decided by the general, who would then report to the king. This is not an empty statement. My great-grandfather said that when Li Mu was a general in Zhao, he allowed the soldiers to use the revenue from the military markets to provide for themselves and reward their comrades. He did not interfere with their decisions. Because he gave them autonomy and held them accountable for success, Li Mu was able to fully utilize his talents and command an army of 1,300 chariots, 3,000 cavalry, and 100,000 soldiers. He defeated the Xiongnu in the north, destroyed the Donghu, conquered the Danlin, suppressed the Qin in the west, and supported the Han and Wei in the south. At that time, Zhao was on the verge of becoming a dominant power. Later, King Zhao Xian ascended the throne, and his mother, the Queen Dowager, was in power. King Zhao Xian appointed Guo Kai, who slandered Li Mu, and Li Mu was eventually executed. Yan Ju replaced him, but the army was defeated in the north, and Zhao was conquered by Qin. Now, I have heard that Wei Shang, the officer in charge of Yunzhong, allows the soldiers to use the revenue from the military markets to provide for themselves and reward their comrades. He also provides them with private funds, and every five days, he slaughters an ox to feed the soldiers and officers. As a result, the Xiongnu avoid Yunzhong and do not come near the border. Once, the Xiongnu invaded, but Wei Shang led his troops to attack them and killed many of them. The soldiers are all local people who have left their fields to join the army. They do not know about military regulations or procedures. They fight all day, killing and capturing enemies, but they do not receive any rewards. The officials enforce the law strictly. I believe that Your Majesty's laws are too strict and the rewards are too small. Furthermore, Wei Shang, the officer in charge of Yunzhong, has been promoted six ranks for his achievements in killing enemies and capturing prisoners. If Your Majesty were to punish him, it would be unfair. Based on this, I believe that even if Your Majesty had Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them effectively. I am a fool, and I have offended Your Majesty. I deserve to be punished!" Emperor Xiaowen was moved by his words. On that day, he ordered Feng Tang to present a decree pardoning Wei Shang and restoring him to his position as the officer in charge of Yunzhong. He also appointed Tang as the commander of the chariots and cavalry, in charge of the Middle Guard and the chariots and soldiers of the various principalities.

Seven years later, Emperor Jing ascended the throne and appointed Tang as the prime minister of the Principality of Chu, but Tang declined. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, he sought out talented individuals and recommended Feng Tang. At that time, Tang was over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so he recommended his son, Feng Sui, as an attendant. Sui, whose courtesy name was Wangsun, was also a talented man and a friend of mine.

The Grand Historian Sima Qian said: Zhang Ji's words about respecting elders and upholding the law without being swayed by personal feelings are wise. Feng Gong's views on the qualities of a good general are insightful. His words, "If you do not know a person, look at his friends," are true. The virtues of these two men are worthy of being remembered. The Book of Rites says, "If one is not biased and does not form factions, the way of the king will be clear and unobstructed; if one is not biased and does not form factions, the way of the king will be easy and smooth." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong embodied these principles. [end of text]


